  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-130233",
    "knowledge_point_id": "215130d1-96ea-5f88-8de0-d73758ea8344",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval Using the Complement",
    "problem": "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{5}),$ compute $P(X\\ge 5).$",
    "worked_solution": "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{5}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{3}{5}), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 5.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{0}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =0.9744.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5) \\\\  & =1-0.9744 \\\\  & =0.0256.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
